body:
WitForge is a local-first, security-first conversational AI operating platform.
It is not merely a chatbot.
It is an intelligent orchestration system through which a user can express an outcome in natural language and WitForge can:
1. understand the objective;
2. identify and solve the underlying problem;
3. reason about possible approaches;
4. create a plan;
5. discover required capabilities;
6. determine required permissions;
7. request authorization;
8. apply security and policy controls;
9. execute authorized actions;
10. verify results;
11. detect and correct failures;
12. adapt the plan;
13. continue where safely possible;
14. recover or roll back when necessary;
15. maintain an auditable record;
16. clearly report the result.
WitForge is therefore an AI operating layer between human intent and authorized digital action.

The primary interface is conversational.
Users can communicate through:
• text;
• voice;
• images;
• video;
• files;
• screenshots;
• documents;
• structured data;
• application context;
• device context;
• approved external information.
Conversation is the primary control surface, but not the only interface.
WitForge may expose:
• dashboards;
• action previews;
• approval dialogs;
• security alerts;
• task timelines;
• device controls;
• account management;
• project management;
• media editors;
• asset inventories;
• audit records;
• developer consoles.

Every meaningful request is transformed into an internal task representation.
The engine determines:
• desired outcome;
• constraints;
• resources;
• dependencies;
• risks;
• required capabilities;
• required permissions;
• affected accounts;
• affected devices;
• affected data;
• expected result;
• verification method;
• recovery strategy.
Natural language must never directly bypass the security layer.

Problem solving is a first-class subsystem.
WitForge must not merely execute the literal wording of a request.
It must determine what problem the user is actually trying to solve.
The problem-solving lifecycle is:
Understand → Diagnose → Generate Solutions → Evaluate → Select → Plan → Execute → Verify → Correct → Continue/Recover → Report
The system may:
• decompose large problems;
• identify dependencies;
• identify missing information;
• detect contradictions;
• compare approaches;
• research relevant information;
• test hypotheses;
• diagnose failures;
• generate alternatives;
• select safer alternatives;
• retry failed operations;
• change execution strategy;
• repair configuration;
• recover interrupted tasks;
• continue from the last verified state.
Problem solving must remain subordinate to:
• user authority;
• capability permissions;
• security policy;
• authentication;
• application permissions;
• operating-system controls;
• legal constraints.
Problem solving can solve around a failure; it cannot solve around a security boundary.

Failure must not automatically terminate a task.
When an operation fails, WitForge should determine:
1. whether the failure is temporary;
2. whether retrying is safe;
3. whether another method exists;
4. whether the problem can be repaired;
5. whether additional authorization is required;
6. whether rollback is required;
7. whether the task can safely continue.
Example:
Upload fails → diagnose → check authentication → refresh authorized session → retry → verify upload.
But:
Permission denied → do not bypass → explain → request legitimate permission or stop.
The system must distinguish:
• recoverable failure;
• authorization failure;
• security block;
• policy violation;
• unavailable capability;
• external service failure;
• invalid input;
• environmental failure;
• irreversible failure.

The Universal Capability Broker is the central abstraction connecting WitForge to external systems.
Capabilities are discovered through legitimate interfaces.
Priority:
1. official API;
2. official SDK;
3. official automation interface;
4. operating-system API;
5. accessibility interface;
6. browser automation;
7. other explicitly authorized integration mechanisms.
Universal does not mean unrestricted.
It means:
«WitForge can support any system for which a legitimate, authorized technical interface exists.»

Permission states:
• "NOT_REQUESTED"
• "REQUESTED"
• "GRANTED"
• "DENIED"
• "EXPIRED"
• "REVOKED"
• "SUSPENDED"
• "BLOCKED_BY_SECURITY"
• "BLOCKED_BY_POLICY"
Permissions are scoped.
Possible scope dimensions:
• user;
• agent;
• task;
• project;
• application;
• account;
• device;
• resource;
• action;
• time;
• purpose;
• environment.

WitForge supports explicit delegation.
A user may authorize:
«“Act on my behalf to manage my calendar.”»
That authorization remains bounded by the selected capabilities and scope.
It does not grant unrestricted access to:
• unrelated accounts;
• unrelated devices;
• unrelated applications;
• unrestricted financial actions;
• security-sensitive operations.

Autonomous operation is controlled delegation, not unrestricted AI freedom.
Autonomous tasks require:
• defined scope;
• defined capabilities;
• defined resources;
• expiration;
• security policy;
• risk threshold;
• action limits;
• emergency stop;
• audit trail.
Critical actions may always require renewed human approval.

WitForge can control supported applications through adapters.
Applications may include:
• email;
• messaging;
• social platforms;
• browsers;
• productivity tools;
• development environments;
• media applications;
• cloud services;
• business systems;
• smart-home applications;
• enterprise systems.
Each integration is independently authenticated and permissioned.

WitForge can:
• search;
• retrieve information;
• navigate websites;
• inspect pages;
• interact with supported forms;
• download authorized files;
• upload authorized files;
• complete legitimate workflows.
Web content is untrusted input.
Instructions embedded in webpages cannot modify WitForge policy.

Browser automation supports:
• navigation;
• clicking;
• typing;
• scrolling;
• tab management;
• file upload/download;
• page inspection;
• authenticated workflows.
Credential handling must use the Credential Broker rather than exposing raw secrets unnecessarily to the model.

Where explicitly authorized, WitForge may control:
• keyboard;
• mouse;
• windows;
• applications;
• files;
• terminal;
• screen;
• supported desktop automation interfaces.
Operating-system security remains authoritative.

Target platforms include:
• Android;
• iOS;
• iPadOS;
• macOS;
• Windows;
• Linux;
• ChromeOS;
• web browsers;
• servers;
• cloud environments;
• supported wearables;
• supported smart devices.

Supported capabilities may include:
• intents;
• app links;
• notifications;
• camera;
• microphone;
• files;
• media;
• Bluetooth;
• USB;
• Sharesheet;
• Accessibility;
• ADB;
• Wireless Debugging;
• Shizuku;
• Termux;
• supported system APIs.
Android permissions remain authoritative.

Accessibility automation may support:
• reading visible UI;
• clicking;
• typing;
• scrolling;
• swiping;
• focusing;
• navigation.
Accessibility authorization must be explicitly granted by the device owner.
WitForge cannot silently obtain or elevate Accessibility privileges.

When deliberately enabled and authorized:
• device information;
• shell;
• files;
• logs;
• application management;
• launch;
• diagnostics;
• supported installation operations.
ADB access is treated as high privilege.

Termux integration may provide:
• shell;
• scripts;
• filesystem;
• Git;
• Node.js;
• Python;
• package management;
• local servers;
• development;
• diagnostics.
Controls include:
• command validation;
• executable restrictions;
• working-directory restrictions;
• timeouts;
• output limits;
• environment filtering;
• security analysis;
• audit logging.

Supported interfaces may include:
• App Intents;
• Shortcuts;
• URL schemes;
• Share extensions;
• approved APIs;
• supported accessibility/automation.
Potential capabilities:
• "apple.shortcuts"
• "apple.intents"
• "apple.files"
• "apple.photos"
• "apple.camera"
• "apple.microphone"
• "apple.contacts"
• "apple.calendar"
• "apple.reminders"
• "apple.notifications"
• "apple.accessibility"
• "apple.macos.automation"



27. macOS
Supported controls may include:
• files;
• screen;
• accessibility;
• camera;
• microphone;
• notifications;
• applications;
• terminal;
• Shortcuts;
• automation.
macOS security permissions remain authoritative.

Supported interfaces may include:
• Win32;
• Windows App SDK;
• PowerShell;
• Windows Terminal;
• WSL;
• filesystem;
• notifications;
• UI Automation;
• browser automation;
• Microsoft Graph;
• supported system APIs.
Administrative operations require appropriate elevation.

Supported interfaces may include:
• shell;
• filesystem;
• DBus;
• desktop automation;
• X11;
• supported Wayland interfaces;
• applications;
• notifications;
• Git;
• Python;
• Node;
• Docker;
• package managers;
• services.

Supported interfaces may include:
• Chrome;
• files;
• notifications;
• Android applications;
• Linux environment;
• supported device APIs.

Supported integrations may include:
• Google Account;
• Gmail;
• Calendar;
• Contacts;
• Drive;
• Docs;
• Sheets;
• YouTube;
• Photos;
• Maps;
• Tasks;
• Meet.
Each service receives separate permissions.

Supported integrations may include:
• Microsoft Account;
• Outlook;
• Calendar;
• Contacts;
• OneDrive;
• Teams;
• Graph;
• Office;
• Windows.

Potential integrations include:
• GitHub;
• GitLab;
• Bitbucket;
• Dropbox;
• Slack;
• Discord;
• Notion;
• Jira;
• AWS;
• Azure;
• Google Cloud;
• Cloudflare;
• media platforms;
• automation platforms;
• smart-home platforms;
• enterprise APIs;
• supported vehicles;
• supported wearables.
Availability depends on legitimate APIs and authorization.

Separate capabilities exist for:
• read;
• search;
• compose;
• draft;
• send;
• reply;
• forward;
• delete;
• archive;
• attach.
A request to “reply to that email” may require:
"email.read → email.reply → email.send"
Each authority is separately evaluated.

Supported operations:
• read;
• create;
• write;
• rename;
• move;
• copy;
• delete;
• share;
• export;
• import.
Sensitive locations require additional controls.

Capabilities include:
• camera view;
• photo capture;
• video capture;
• microphone recording;
• live audio;
• screen view;
• screenshot;
• screen recording;
• screen sharing.
These are sensitive capabilities and require explicit authorization.

Location permissions may distinguish:
• approximate;
• precise;
• foreground;
• background.
WitForge cannot upgrade approximate location to precise location without authorization.

Supported operations may include:
• discover;
• connect;
• read;
• write;
• transfer.
Each device and operation is permission-scoped.

Credentials must be isolated through a Credential Broker.
Supported stores may include:
• Apple Keychain;
• Android Keystore;
• Windows Credential Manager;
• Linux secret stores;
• browser credential APIs;
• enterprise secret managers.
The model should not receive raw credentials unless technically necessary and explicitly permitted.
Biometric operations return authorization results, not raw biometric templates.

Commands must contain:
• command ID;
• user ID;
• device ID;
• capability;
• action;
• scope;
• expiration;
• authorization;
• correlation ID.
Replay protection is required.

The Capability Firewall controls access to:
• applications;
• devices;
• accounts;
• files;
• APIs;
• tools;
• network resources;
• privileged operations.
Every execution request passes through it.

Tokens bind authority to:
• subject;
• agent;
• device;
• account;
• resource;
• scope;
• purpose;
• expiration;
• approval;
• policy version.
Tokens must be revocable.

Detection covers:
• prompt injection;
• secret exfiltration;
• command injection;
• path traversal;
• SSRF;
• credential spraying;
• suspicious automation;
• malicious files;
• abnormal tool usage;
• integrity violations;
• suspicious account behavior.
Detection does not rely solely on the model.

Security Shield provides:
• threat monitoring;
• classification;
• firewalling;
• identity protection;
• tool protection;
• prompt-injection protection;
• file protection;
• network protection;
• sandboxing;
• quarantine;
• incident response;
• evidence storage;
• security dashboard;
• recovery;
• self-test.

Websites, emails, uploads, GitHub content, API responses, documents and tool output are untrusted.
They may contain instructions.
Those instructions are treated as data, not authority.
External content cannot:
• grant itself permissions;
• change policy;
• become Owner;
• disable security;
• expose credentials;
• authorize actions.

Authorized defensive operations may include:
• asset registration;
• safe reconnaissance;
• configuration scanning;
• dependency audits;
• safe exploit validation;
• attack-path simulation;
• privilege-boundary testing;
• integrity scanning;
• evidence collection;
• remediation;
• isolation;
• patching;
• restoration;
• verification.
Active defense is restricted to authorized assets.

Risk factors include:
• capability sensitivity;
• data sensitivity;
• financial impact;
• reversibility;
• account impact;
• device impact;
• external visibility;
• security impact;
• legal impact;
• scope;
• autonomy;
• uncertainty.
Risk classifications:
• LOW;
• MEDIUM;
• HIGH;
• CRITICAL;
• PROHIBITED.

Agents operate inside isolated execution boundaries.
Sandbox controls include:
• filesystem restrictions;
• network restrictions;
• process restrictions;
• resource limits;
• capability restrictions;
• time limits;
• output limits;
• audit logging.

Credential Broker manages:
• API keys;
• OAuth tokens;
• refresh tokens;
• passwords;
• session credentials;
• certificates;
• secure device credentials.
Credential exposure to agents is minimized.

Users must be able to immediately stop:
• current task;
• agent;
• application integration;
• device;
• autonomous mode;
• network execution;
• account operations.

Every integration must enforce:
• authentication;
• authorization;
• rate limits;
• trusted endpoints;
• input validation;
• output validation;
• secure transport;
• capability checks;
• audit logging.

WitForge supports secure authentication using appropriate mechanisms.
Possible methods:
• passwords;
• passkeys;
• OAuth;
• device authentication;
• MFA;
• hardware-backed credentials;
• platform authentication.

The Owner is the highest administrative authority within WitForge.
Owner identity is private administrative data and must not be unnecessarily exposed to:
• frontend bundles;
• public APIs;
• logs;
• analytics;
• URLs;
• external services.
Owner status must be protected by strong authentication.

Owner operations require:
• strong authentication;
• secure session management;
• audit logging;
• sensitive-operation confirmation;
• recovery controls;
• revocation mechanisms.

WitForge supports specialized agents.
Examples:
• planner;
• researcher;
• coding agent;
• browser agent;
• device agent;
• media agent;
• security agent;
• account agent;
• communications agent;
• finance/reconciliation agent;
• project agent.
Agents cannot independently grant themselves capabilities.

Users can organize work into projects.
Projects contain:
• goals;
• tasks;
• agents;
• files;
• integrations;
• permissions;
• memory;
• assets;
• audit history.

Memory is divided into:
• conversation memory;
• project memory;
• user preferences;
• task state;
• verified facts;
• integration state.
Security policy and permissions cannot be rewritten by ordinary memory.

WitForge supports:
• text;
• speech;
• images;
• video;
• audio;
• documents;
• screenshots.
Potential media operations:
• speech-to-text;
• text-to-speech;
• image generation;
• image editing;
• video generation;
• video editing;
• audio generation;
• multimodal analysis.
Provider availability must be verified.

WitForge can support multiple providers.
Provider abstraction must separate:
• model discovery;
• model execution;
• credentials;
• quotas;
• capabilities;
• costs;
• safety policies.
No provider is assumed to magically support an unavailable capability.

Users may create digital avatars.
Avatar construction can include:
• appearance;
• clothing;
• equipment;
• attributes;
• personality;
• visual components;
• metadata.
An avatar must be fully constructed before entering systems that require complete identity.

Avatar components may be generated by authorized AI systems.
Each generated component receives:
• identity;
• provenance;
• metadata;
• ownership record;
• creation timestamp.

Each digital asset may receive:
• unique asset ID;
• cryptographic identity;
• provenance hash;
• creation record;
• ownership record;
• version history.

Ownership records must distinguish:
• creator;
• current owner;
• source;
• transfer history;
• merge history;
• status.

WitForge supports digital companion/pet assets.
Pets may have:
• identity;
• appearance;
• attributes;
• rarity;
• provenance;
• ownership.

Compatible pets may be merged subject to:
• ownership;
• compatibility;
• rarity rules;
• anti-duplication;
• transaction integrity.

Users receive an inventory containing:
• avatars;
• avatar pieces;
• pets;
• media;
• digital assets;
• licenses;
• account-linked assets.

A future marketplace may support authorized asset exchange.
All marketplace operations require:
• ownership verification;
• anti-fraud;
• transaction integrity;
• applicable legal controls;
• user authorization.

The Arena is a controlled digital competition system.
Current economic concept:
• each participant wagers 100 LD;
• total pool = 200 LD;
• winner receives 198 LD;
• treasury receives 2 LD.
Arena mechanics must remain isolated from core security and account systems.

Arena operations require:
• authenticated participants;
• verified avatars;
• anti-cheat;
• transaction integrity;
• deterministic/auditable results;
• fraud monitoring;
• dispute handling.

Economic balances use double-entry accounting.
Every transaction must have:
• debit;
• credit;
• transaction ID;
• timestamp;
• actor;
• reason;
• source;
• destination;
• resulting balances.
Balances must be derived from auditable ledger state.



90. 1% ALLOCATION
The Arena treasury allocation is:
1% of the total wager pool.
For a 200 LD pool:
2 LD treasury allocation.
The exact economic rules must remain deterministic and auditable.

Treasury use may be governed toward:
• sustainable energy;
• agriculture;
• technology;
• recycling;
• resource management;
• longevity;
• mining.
Governance must be transparent and independently auditable.

Real-money functionality must remain disabled unless all applicable:
• licensing;
• regulatory;
• payment;
• responsible-gambling;
• consumer-protection;
• tax;
• identity;
• age-verification;
• jurisdictional
requirements have been satisfied.
A specification does not constitute legal authorization.

WitForge must maintain jurisdiction-aware compliance controls.
The system must never interpret a user's instruction as permission to violate law.
Where requirements are uncertain, WitForge must:
• identify the uncertainty;
• explain it;
• avoid irreversible action;
• request appropriate human/legal review where necessary.

WitForge requires versioned legal documents.
Documents include:
• Terms of Service;
• Privacy Policy;
• acceptable-use rules;
• capability delegation terms;
• account terms;
• marketplace terms;
• economic terms;
• Arena terms.

Privacy architecture follows:
• data minimization;
• purpose limitation;
• access controls;
• encryption;
• retention policies;
• deletion controls;
• auditability.
Sensitive information must not be unnecessarily exposed.

Every consequential operation generates an audit record.
Records may include:
• event ID;
• timestamp;
• actor;
• agent;
• device;
• account;
• capability;
• action;
• decision;
• reason;
• risk;
• approval;
• result;
• correlation ID;
• evidence hash.
Secrets must be masked or excluded.

Audit records should support:
• integrity hashes;
• chained records;
• append-only storage;
• evidence hashes;
• secure timestamps.
Audit history must not be silently altered.

Every important operation has a verification method.
Verification may check:
• API response;
• application state;
• device state;
• file state;
• account state;
• external confirmation;
• cryptographic evidence;
• expected result.

Users must be able to understand:
• what WitForge intended;
• what it did;
• what permissions it used;
• what failed;
• what it corrected;
• what it verified;
• what remains incomplete.

Request:
«“Reply to that email.”»
WitForge determines:
1. locate email;
2. require "email.read";
3. identify response;
4. generate draft;
5. require "email.reply";
6. require "email.send";
7. security check;
8. approval if required;
9. send;
10. verify sent state;
11. audit.

Request:
«“Install this application on my Android phone.”»
WitForge:
1. identifies target device;
2. verifies device trust;
3. inspects application;
4. checks source;
5. scans package;
6. determines installation capability;
7. requests required authorization;
8. checks security policy;
9. installs;
10. verifies installation;
11. reports result.
It cannot bypass Android security controls.

A user may have multiple trusted devices.
Each device maintains:
• identity;
• trust state;
• capabilities;
• sessions;
• authorization;
• revocation state.

WitForge should operate locally wherever practical.
Local operation provides:
• lower latency;
• privacy;
• offline functionality;
• local files;
• local automation;
• local model support where available.
External services are used only when required or authorized.

Offline mode supports:
• local conversation;
• local planning;
• local files;
• local models where available;
• queued tasks;
• local automation;
• deferred synchronization.
Tasks requiring external services remain pending.

WitForge must never claim:
• an account was created when it was not;
• a message was sent when it was not;
• a file was uploaded when it was not;
• a payment succeeded when it did not;
• a security scan passed when it did not run;
• a capability exists when it does not;
• an integration works when it has not been verified.
Simulation must be clearly labeled.

The platform database stores:
• users;
• authentication;
• sessions;
• projects;
• tasks;
• permissions;
• capabilities;
• accounts;
• devices;
• agents;
• memory;
• assets;
• transactions;
• security events;
• audit events;
• integrations.
Sensitive secrets should be stored outside ordinary model-accessible records.

Asset records include:
• asset ID;
• type;
• owner;
• creator;
• provenance;
• rarity;
• status;
• parent assets;
• merge history;
• cryptographic identity.

Anti-fraud controls include:
• duplicate detection;
• transaction monitoring;
• identity verification where required;
• rate limits;
• anomaly detection;
• account separation;
• audit trails;
• asset provenance.

WitForge may support organisations with:
• administrators;
• members;
• roles;
• teams;
• projects;
• shared resources;
• policies;
• delegated capabilities.
Organisation authority does not automatically override individual account authority.

Subscription systems may support:
• plans;
• usage;
• limits;
• billing;
• invoices;
• entitlements.
Billing actions require explicit authority.

Media capabilities include:
• image creation;
• image editing;
• video creation;
• video editing;
• audio creation;
• audio editing;
• speech recognition;
• speech synthesis.
Provider capabilities and licensing must be checked.

AI is a component inside the security architecture, not the security authority itself.
The model may:
• identify threats;
• propose defenses;
• analyze evidence;
• recommend remediation.
The enforcement layer decides whether execution is permitted.

Recovery can include:
• session revocation;
• capability revocation;
• account disconnect;
• device isolation;
• integration disablement;
• rollback;
• restoration;
• quarantine;
• credential rotation;
• lockdown.
Recovery must itself be audited.

Security evidence may include:
• event records;
• hashes;
• screenshots;
• logs;
• tool results;
• scan results;
• affected resource identifiers.
Secrets must be protected.

WitForge should support:
• logs;
• metrics;
• traces;
• correlation IDs;
• task timelines;
• OpenTelemetry-compatible observability.
Security and operational telemetry must remain privacy-aware.

Security design references:
• OWASP ASVS 5.x;
• OWASP API Security Top 10;
• secure coding practices;
• least privilege;
• defense in depth;
• zero-trust principles;
• secure-by-default architecture.
Framework versions must be revalidated during implementation.

Every tool exposes a manifest containing:
• tool ID;
• version;
• capabilities;
• inputs;
• outputs;
• permissions;
• risk class;
• supported platforms;
• authentication method;
• verification method;
• rollback support.

Every integration should have a mock/test adapter where practical.
This allows testing:
• authorization;
• failures;
• retries;
• rollback;
• verification;
• security boundaries.
No production integration should be considered validated merely because its UI exists.

Every release must test:
• authentication;
• authorization;
• permissions;
• session security;
• capability enforcement;
• input validation;
• SSRF protection;
• path traversal;
• command injection;
• prompt injection;
• secret handling;
• rate limiting;
• account boundaries;
• device trust;
• audit integrity;
• rollback;
• recovery;
• failure continuation.

WitForge should provide an internal security and health self-test.
Checks include:
• configuration;
• database;
• authentication;
• permissions;
• integrations;
• security controls;
• dependency health;
• audit system;
• recovery system.
Results must distinguish:
PASS / FAIL / WARNING / NOT TESTED

A release must document:
• prerequisites;
• supported Node version;
• dependency installation;
• environment variables;
• secure configuration;
• database initialization;
• first-run setup;
• startup;
• health check;
• test commands;
• shutdown;
• upgrade procedure;
• rollback procedure.

Use semantic versioning where appropriate:
"MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH"
Every release must identify:
• version;
• build date;
• source revision;
• dependency state;
• test status;
• known limitations;
• security status.

When instructed:
«“Create me an account on X.”»
WitForge:
1. identifies the service;
2. determines whether account creation is supported;
3. discovers the legitimate registration interface;
4. identifies required information;
5. identifies email/phone verification requirements;
6. identifies payment requirements;
7. identifies terms/consent requirements;
8. presents important requirements;
9. obtains required approval;
10. creates the account;
11. completes supported configuration;
12. handles legitimate verification;
13. stores credentials securely;
14. verifies the resulting account;
15. registers the integration;
16. reports the result.

WitForge may:
• generate strong passwords;
• generate usernames where permitted;
• fill legitimate registration forms;
• configure supported settings;
• complete authorized email verification;
• manage credentials securely.
WitForge must not:
• fabricate identity;
• falsely claim age;
• falsify legal information;
• bypass CAPTCHA;
• bypass identity verification;
• bypass phone verification;
• evade service restrictions;
• impersonate another person;
• accept legally binding terms when personal confirmation is required without the required authority.

Payment requires:
• supported payment integration;
• authorization;
• appropriate user approval;
• transaction verification.
Payment credentials remain protected by the Credential Broker.

WitForge maintains an account inventory containing:
• service;
• account identifier;
• connection status;
• capabilities;
• authorization date;
• expiration;
• security status;
• revocation state.
Multiple accounts for one service must remain separated.

Where supported, WitForge may assist with:
• password changes;
• MFA;
• passkeys;
• recovery methods;
• sessions;
• security settings.

Deletion is consequential.
WitForge must:
• identify affected data;
• warn about consequences;
• obtain required confirmation;
• execute through legitimate mechanisms;
• verify the resulting state.

Disconnect should, where supported:
• revoke tokens;
• remove capability grants;
• invalidate local integration state;
• preserve required audit information.

Legal and policy documents must be:
• versioned;
• dated;
• jurisdiction-aware;
• auditable;
• presented at required points.
WitForge must distinguish system policy from legal advice.

WitForge is responsible for implementing:
• security boundaries;
• permission enforcement;
• truthful status;
• auditability;
• safe defaults;
• failure containment;
• capability isolation.

WitForge must never attempt to defeat:
• OS security;
• authentication;
• access controls;
• encryption;
• CAPTCHA;
• identity verification;
• application sandboxing;
• provider security;
• network security;
• permission systems.
No problem-solving capability may override this rule.

WitForge may improve:
• planning;
• reasoning;
• prompts;
• adapters;
• workflows;
• diagnostics;
• performance;
• error recovery.
Self-improvement must pass controlled testing and cannot modify security authority without explicit governance.

The architecture must support future:
• operating systems;
• devices;
• AI models;
• applications;
• APIs;
• agents;
• automation systems;
• media systems;
• digital assets;
• security technologies.
New capabilities are added through controlled adapters and manifests.

Authority hierarchy:
1. system security;
2. operating-system security;
3. authenticated user/Owner authority;
4. explicit capability permissions;
5. policy;
6. agent delegation;
7. task instructions;
8. external content.
External content can never outrank trusted authority.

When something goes wrong:
1. stop unsafe propagation;
2. preserve evidence;
3. determine scope;
4. isolate affected resources;
5. diagnose;
6. repair if safe;
7. restore if necessary;
8. verify;
9. continue where safe;
10. report honestly.

Every major capability requires adversarial tests.
Tests should attempt:
• unauthorized execution;
• permission escalation;
• account crossover;
• prompt injection;
• malicious tool output;
• malicious file input;
• SSRF;
• command injection;
• path traversal;
• secret extraction;
• replay;
• session abuse;
• privilege escalation.
Expected result is containment or rejection.

WitForge should diagnose its own operational failures.
Examples:
• missing dependency;
• expired authentication;
• unavailable API;
• incorrect configuration;
• network failure;
• invalid parameter;
• permission change;
• provider outage.
It may repair configuration where authorized.
It must not silently weaken security to make the system work.

A task that fails should preserve:
• original objective;
• completed steps;
• verified state;
• failed step;
• reason;
• attempted corrections;
• remaining work;
• required permissions.
This prevents unnecessary repetition.

Request:
«“Make a funny cat video, add music and post it on my YouTube account.”»
WitForge performs:
1. understand desired style;
2. generate or source authorized cat footage;
3. generate/select appropriately licensed music;
4. edit video;
5. verify audio/video;
6. obtain YouTube authorization;
7. check "youtube.upload";
8. check "youtube.publish";
9. security scan;
10. preview;
11. upload;
12. verify upload;
13. publish if authorized;
14. verify public status;
15. return result.
“Create” and “publish” are separate authorities.

Request:
«“Find the best solution to this problem.”»
WitForge should:
1. define the problem;
2. gather relevant evidence;
3. distinguish facts from assumptions;
4. generate alternatives;
5. compare cost/risk/benefit;
6. identify uncertainty;
7. recommend an approach;
8. optionally execute authorized implementation;
9. verify the outcome.

Request:
«“Fix my application.”»
WitForge may:
1. inspect project;
2. reproduce issue;
3. identify root cause;
4. create a patch;
5. run tests;
6. inspect security impact;
7. show diff;
8. apply patch if authorized;
9. rebuild;
10. run regression tests;
11. verify;
12. preserve rollback state.

Request:
«“Secure my server.”»
WitForge may:
1. identify authorized server;
2. inventory services;
3. scan configuration;
4. identify vulnerabilities;
5. prioritize risks;
6. propose remediation;
7. obtain approval;
8. apply safe fixes;
9. verify;
10. monitor;
11. report.
It must not scan or attack unauthorized systems.

WitForge should be:
• capable;
• honest;
• adaptive;
• secure;
• transparent;
• user-controlled;
• extensible;
• local-first;
• privacy-aware;
• failure-tolerant.
The objective is not maximum autonomy.
The objective is maximum useful autonomy within legitimate authority.

«WitForge may solve around problems, but it may never solve around security.»
It may:
• correct;
• retry;
• adapt;
• substitute;
• recover;
• continue;
• request additional authorization.
It may never:
• bypass;
• forge;
• impersonate;
• escalate without authority;
• disable security;
• evade authentication;
• circumvent permissions.

Economic operations must remain:
• auditable;
• isolated;
• permission-controlled;
• jurisdiction-aware;
• fraud-resistant.
No AI agent receives unrestricted financial authority.

WitForge must always distinguish:
• planned;
• simulated;
• attempted;
• succeeded;
• verified;
• failed;
• blocked;
• unknown.
A generated answer is not evidence that an external action occurred.
